    <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="overview-title">
      <div class="container">
        <h2 class="case-section__title reveal" id="overview-title">Project Overview</h2>
        <p class="case-section__text reveal">
          <strong>What it is.</strong> Fire Before Fire is an ESP32-based early-warning
          system that monitors electrical load current and temperature to flag hazardous
          heating <em>before</em> ignition — combining physics-derived features, a live
          threshold rule engine, and an on-device ML ensemble.
        </p>
        <p class="case-section__text reveal">
          <strong>Who it is for.</strong> Prototype research and educational demos around
          electrical fire-risk sensing — not a certified fire-safety product. Useful for
          exploring edge ML, sensor fusion, and cloud-synced model updates on constrained hardware.
        </p>
        <p class="case-section__text reveal">
          <strong>Overall goal.</strong> Ship a working SoftAP dashboard + optional cloud
          pipeline where the board can alert locally (buzzer/relay, in-app WebSocket) and
          sync labeled batches to Render for GNB + softmax logistic regression refit.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section case-section--alt" aria-labelledby="problem-title">
      <div class="container">
        <h2 class="case-section__title reveal" id="problem-title">Problem Statement</h2>
        <p class="case-section__text reveal">
          Electrical fires often build from abnormal heating and current draw before flames
          appear. Many consumer detectors only react after smoke or heat thresholds are
          crossed. An edge device that scores risk from live current/temperature features —
          with clear local alerts and optional phone notifications — can surface caution
          earlier in the failure curve.
        </p>
        <ul class="case-list reveal">
          <li><strong>AC current sensing</strong> — naive ADC averages collapse toward DC bias; true RMS sampling is required</li>
          <li><strong>Sensor drift</strong> — ACS712 zero-point shifts over time and needs idle re-zeroing</li>
          <li><strong>Label leakage</strong> — adaptive alert thresholds must not rewrite training labels</li>
          <li><strong>Edge constraints</strong> — models must fit on ESP32 flash while still syncing with cloud refits</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="solution-title">
      <div class="container">
        <h2 class="case-section__title reveal" id="solution-title">Solution</h2>
        <p class="case-section__text reveal">
          The firmware reads an ACS712 on GPIO 34 (true RMS over ≥1 mains cycle) and a
          DS18B20 on GPIO 4, derives heating features (slopes, variance, power estimate,
          temperature acceleration), and scores risk with:
        </p>
        <ul class="case-list reveal">
          <li>A <strong>threshold rule engine</strong> for live alerts (always on)</li>
          <li>On-device <strong>Gaussian Naive Bayes</strong> when the labeled dataset is sufficient</li>
          <li><strong>Softmax logistic regression</strong> weights from LittleFS / cloud online refit</li>
          <li>A <strong>confidence-weighted ensemble</strong> that can override rules when confident</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="case-section__text reveal">
          A SoftAP dashboard (`FireBeforeFire`) is served from LittleFS. With home Wi‑Fi and
          an API key saved once, the node uploads batches to Render, which refits GNB + LR;
          the ESP pulls updated models and can trigger OS Web Push notifications.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

